contentText:
Kacey Musgraves had the best time during her two-night stand at New York&#8217;s Madison Square Garden, including on Tuesday night (Sept. 1), when she finally got to sing her &#8220;Easy&#8221; duet with Troye Sivan live on stage for the first time. The Australian singer was a surprise guest during the second night of Musgraves&#8217; MSG stand, popping out to perform the tune in brown leather pants with silver rivets down the front and a brown plaid shirt under a chocolate-covered vest.

The two circled each other on stage , singing, &#8220;I can&#8217;t even look at you/ Would you look at the space just next to your feet?/ The wood is warping/ The lines distorting/ This house is on fire, woo!/ Burning the tears right off my face/ What the hell did we do?/ Tell me we&#8217;ll make it through&#8221; as the crowd howled in excitement.

The onstage team-up comes nearly six years after Musgraves and Sivan dropped a remix of &#8220;Easy&#8221; featuring producer Mark Ronson and a 2020 Bardia Zeinali-directed video in which the pair played a couple of friends on the run. Sivan released the original version of &#8220;Easy&#8221; on his August 2020 In a Dream EP. The pair have a long history of collaborations, first getting together in 2019 for &#8220;Glittery,&#8221; the high-energy duet from The Kacey Musgraves Christmas Show special, then again for &#8220;Easy&#8221; and now for the live debut of the song.

Sivan posted a fun backstage clip of their collab on TikTok cued to his song &#8220;She&#8217;s the Best,&#8221; in which the pair struck poses and slow danced next to each other while mouthing the lyrics &#8220;that girl, that girl, that girl, that girl, that girl.&#8221;

The night before, Musgraves paid tribute to late country icon Dolly Parton , who died last week at 80 . Musgraves called Parton a &#8220;supernova of a person&#8221; and said she was &#8220;never gonna be over&#8221; the country legend&#8217;s death before performing a lively cover of Parton&#8217;s 1981 Billboard Hot 100 No. 1 hit &#8220;9 to 5.&#8221;

Musgraves&#8217; Middle of Nowhere tour will move on to Philadelphia&#8217;s Xfinity Mobile Arena on Friday (Sept. 4).
